<h2>1121 and Pusa basmati from India</h2>
<p>India supplies roughly 80% of the world's traded basmati rice, and the trade concentrates on a handful of varieties. 1121 is the benchmark for extra-long grain — averaging 8.30–8.40mm before cooking, with the post-cooking elongation that Middle Eastern biryani and pulao cooking demands. Pusa varieties (1509, 1401) sit slightly shorter and lower-priced, and cover a large share of volume demand.</p>

<p>We supply raw, steam, sella and golden sella in both, sortex-cleaned, with aging specified to contract.</p>

<h2>How do I specify basmati properly?</h2>
<p>"Basmati, good quality" is not a specification. The parameters that actually determine what lands in your warehouse are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Variety</strong> — 1121 vs Pusa vs traditional. This is the biggest price driver.</li>
  <li><strong>Processing</strong> — raw, steam, sella or golden sella. These cook differently and are not interchangeable.</li>
  <li><strong>Average grain length</strong> — stated in mm, before cooking.</li>
  <li><strong>Broken percentage</strong> — 1%, 2% or 5% are the common contract levels.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — ≤12–13%. Higher moisture means you're paying for water and risking storage problems.</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — the percentage of the stated variety in the lot, typically 92–95%.</li>
  <li><strong>Aging</strong> — 12 or 24 months, if required.</li>
</ul>
<p>Give us those seven and we can quote precisely. Leave them vague and every quote you receive from any exporter will be comparing different things.</p>

